y obvious factor and it's  probably more or more overwhelming than  the rest of this that is the fact that  tuition rates have gone up so much  that now only the rich or the relatively  rich have access to pay the tuition fees  at the universities an example i went to  the  state university of new york one of the  campuses upstate when i went thousand  years ago the cost was practically zero  today that same university state  university supported by taxpayers costs  ten thousand dollars a year  at that time i couldn't if uh what i had  was nothing i could not have attended  the university so the rising tuition  rates i think fits into this outsole  right i mean you're right uh  high tuition rates obviously are a big  problem i don't spend a lot of time on  them in my book because  i don't have a lot new to say because  it's so obvious they're a problem um i  would say that of course for the it  really hurts the middle class right in  that in that if you're very low income  at least the elite schools uh you know  will give you  a lot of grants  there may be some loans but some schools  are saying they i think it's all grants  like like princeton uh but clearly if  you have a loan burden  that's uh that makes uh constricts your  choices for later life i'd also say that  you know colleges they love to boast  about being need blind  and that's true in a sense but they're  they're not wealth blind in that  obviously they reserve slots for the  wealthy so what that essentially means  is that with any individual student that  they accept who's low income they will  provide the the money to go to school  but they don't have room for a lot of  low-income students because they're  committed to admitting so many wealthy  students  so in the big picture they're very  neat and wealth conscious i would say  yes  successes of challenges to affirmative  action policies like in gratz and baccy  i was just wondering to what extent  these preferences have been legally  challenged and whether or not you think  they could be brought in a court  well that's yeah that's an interesting  question  this issue has come up a number of times  such as in the michigan case the  question being for those of you who  didn't hear it can you legally challenge  these kinds of preferences for the  wealthy  the answer seems to be no the reason  being that affirmative action is liable  to legal challenge because it's on the  surface of racial preference and the  constitution i'm not a lawyer but i know  the constitution is very strict about  the circumstances in which you can  favor one race over another  preferences like legacy preference or  development preference are not  on the surface racial preferences  because you could have a minority legacy  there are some children of minority uh  graduates  you could have a minority development  case or athlete in these sports so  the courts have pretty consistently held  that these are not racial preferences  that's kind of the difference between  league you know legalism and reality  because in reality you know legacies at  these schools are overwhelmingly white  and development cases are too and so are  the athletes in these sports if if you  remember that the duke lacrosse case a  few months ago uh people were shocked  that 46 out of the 47  players on the duke men's lacrosse team  turned out to be white and the three who  were  indicted  uh or at least charged i forget if they  were indicted we're all from private  schools you know expensive private high  schools and this is the pattern in these  in these sports actually that there's  overwhelming  overwhelmingly white and so uh you know  there's a gap between reality and uh law  and  this gentleman over here had asked a  question that bears on yours which is  what can students do about this  there's not really a legal remedy but  you know there have been political  attempts over the years to try and uh  restrict or get rid of uh some of these  preferences senator kennedy who in fact  the harvard legacy himself  filed uh a bill kind of a sunshine bill  to bring more information about legacy  preference to light a couple years ago  it didn't go anywhere  partly because and this is one of the  problems i mean all the polls show that  the american public is uh heavily  opposed to legacy preference the  american public wants equality and  admissions but uh  the trouble is that the elites all  benefit from these preferences so you  know for example in the last 20 30 years  there's been a huge growth in earmarked  funding from congress so what happens is  a politician will work the system so  that five million dollars or ten million  dollars or twenty million dollars goes  to a particular school for  you know a new laboratory a new uh a new  building a new project  and as i document my book you know lo  and behold a couple years later that  politicians children go to that college  or the children of a key constituent or  a key staff member so politicians  benefit from this system they work it  they get favors just like businessmen  just like uh  entertainers and celebrities just like  alumni of all kinds who are well off  so uh  the problem is is that it's very hard  for the people to make their will known  over the the voices of the elites on  this issue  yes  at yale for example over half of  undergraduates come from public schools  so students from lower and middle income  backgrounds are getting in do you think  that our society has anything  to benefit from these types of students  socializing with and and being educated  with those more acquainted with the  culture of power and influence  i see your point i think i don't know  the yale statistics i do remember uh  hearing at harvard that 55 of the  students are from public schools and 45  are from private schools so that's  obviously a much greater private school  representation than you would have among  all high school graduates so it's still  heavily weighted toward private schools  in uh in proportional terms and i would  imagine yale is somewhat the same way i  also think that the other 55 who go to  public school come from public schools  and again i'm talking i know harvard  better than yale  that doesn't necessarily mean they're  lower middle income because a lot of  them are coming from  excellent suburban public high schools  whether in you know scarsdale or lake  forest or illinois or or you know  suburban suburbia all over so so i  wouldn't say that 55 percent of them are  coming from you know low-income  households but uh  as to whether there's a benefit from  mixing with the children of leaders  i mean if you're a yale undergraduate  you tell me i mean my experience and  what people have told me is there may  not be that much mixing you know there's  i almost felt there was kind of  uh and people have told me that there's  almost two worlds at some of the elite  colleges  where uh  you know for example at princeton with  its uh eating clubs or harvard with its  finals clubs those are predominantly  where the children of the rich join and  network and i know at princeton for  example asian american students have  complained about being shut out of the  eating club so  i don't know if they would benefit if  there's a lot of mingling  but i'm not sure there really is that  much  interconnection and  exchange of ideas and opening up of  minds and that kind of thing  but you might know better than me  because it's been a long time since i  was in college  you were asked before if you had heard  from any of the administrators have you  heard from any of the parents or  students  well uh i've heard from a few of them on  a  you know  background basis where i don't feel free  to divulge their names but i've had a  number of uh students of wealth and  privilege saying you know you were right  about me  you know and i feel guilty about it i'm  going to work later in life to display a  social conscience so  uh obviously that's not the universal  view i'm sure  many of them are  not too happy with my book  but uh  you know in a way  when the journal articles came out uh  there was more of a battle you know  because my approach then was was new and  fresh and uh  unproven  and i think uh  you know once my article articles came  out you know i won a pulitzer for them  i think  it was sort of generally conceded that  at least i had the right to explore this  terrain  and so i haven't heard a lot of uh  protests but  you know i'm sure they're out there and  there are places where i would not  exactly be made welcome where i  to go knock on the door  well thank you all very much i've  enjoyed it very much nice talking to you  [Applause]  daniel golden was awarded the pulitzer  prize in 2004 for his reports on  preferences for privileged applicants at  elite colleges  currently he's deputy bureau chief at  the wall street journal's boston bureau  you can find the wall street
 

